Chapter: TowerCheck
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Paint
  cites Safari chapter Tower
  cites Judge chapter Grade
  cites Gold chapter TowerGold

 Grade the Tower port against wasm/tower.zig.

 THE BEACON IS THE FIRST NON-POLYGON COMMAND IN THIS PORT. Tag 3 is a disc --
 [3][color][x][y][r][alpha] -- and it is the only command on the wire that is not
 opaque, which is why paint.zig gives it its own tag rather than letting it ride
 the solid path whose colour word has no alpha. It still fits one DrawCmd: the
 alpha is `strength` and x, y, r ride in `pts`. So the streams below carry a
 count of 3 where a quad carries 4, and that difference is graded exactly.

 THE PER-CASE COUNT IS THE STRUCTURAL ASSERTION. Two of the seven cases must draw
 NOTHING -- a tower sunk below the horizon by its own ground drop, and one nearer
 than the near plane -- and a total would hide that. The others are 33, 33, 20 and
 5: 33 is four legs, three rings of four, two brace stages of eight, and the
 beacon; 20 and 5 are what is left after the ground drop eats the lower stages,
 which is the branch that skips a ring or starts a leg part-way up.

 THE NEGATIVE PHASE IS REAL BUT DOES NOT GRADE THE MODULO, and saying so matters
 more than the case does. tower.zig's beacon is a pure function of the step clock
 so it freezes on pause and RUNS BACKWARDS ON REVERSE, so a negative phase is a
 state the game actually reaches, and case seven is case two with the phase
 negated. What it does NOT do is distinguish a floored modulo from a truncated
 one: tower.zig's `@mod(@mod(p, P) + P, P)` is semantics-agnostic by construction,
 and swapping Num's floored mod-real for the truncated remainder leaves this whole
 check green. Measured, after an earlier draft of this paragraph claimed the
 opposite. The port uses floored because that is what @mod means, not because
 anything here would catch it.

 We say:

Section: The Beacon Clock

  offset-ns : List Integer
  offset-ns = [0, 1, 2, 3, 7, 12, 40, 119, 120, 121]

Section: Placing a Tower

 The probe hands the port its four base corners already in the rider frame, which
 is what render.zig does. With the segment origin at zero the map is a shift.

  corners-of : Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List RiderPt
  corners-of (fwd) (right) (yaw) (k) =
    if k >= 4 then []
    else corner-one fwd right yaw k

  corner-one : Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List RiderPt
  corner-one (fwd) (right) (yaw) (k) =
    let ax = base-corner-ax k 0.0 0.0 yaw
    in [RiderPt { right = right + ax.x, forward = fwd + ax.a }] & corners-of fwd right yaw (k + 1)

  tower-cmds : Real, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  tower-cmds (fwd) (right) (yaw) (phase) =
    draw-flat (corners-of fwd right yaw 0) (RiderPt { right = right, forward = fwd }) focal camera-w phase

  case-near : List DrawCmd
  case-near = tower-cmds 14.0 9.0 0.4 60.0

  case-mid : List DrawCmd
  case-mid = tower-cmds 120.0 (0.0 - 30.0) 0.0 30.0

  case-far : List DrawCmd
  case-far = tower-cmds 900.0 60.0 1.1 0.0

  case-deeper : List DrawCmd
  case-deeper = tower-cmds 1700.0 0.0 0.0 90.0

  case-sunk : List DrawCmd
  case-sunk = tower-cmds 3000.0 0.0 0.0 60.0

  case-behind : List DrawCmd
  case-behind = tower-cmds 0.2 0.0 0.0 60.0

  case-neg : List DrawCmd
  case-neg = tower-cmds 120.0 (0.0 - 30.0) 0.0 (0.0 - 45.0)

  all-cmds : List DrawCmd
  all-cmds = case-near & case-mid & case-far & case-deeper & case-sunk & case-behind & case-neg

  per-case : List Integer
  per-case = [list-length case-near, list-length case-mid, list-length case-far, list-length case-deeper, list-length case-sunk, list-length case-behind, list-length case-neg]

Section: Streams

  cmd-tags : List DrawCmd, Integer -> List Integer
  cmd-tags (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else [(list-at cs i).tag] & cmd-tags cs (i + 1)

  cmd-colors : List DrawCmd, Integer -> List Integer
  cmd-colors (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else [(list-at cs i).color] & cmd-colors cs (i + 1)

 A quad reports 4 and a beacon reports 3, because a beacon's pts hold x, y and r
 rather than an even run of coordinates.

  count-of : DrawCmd -> Integer
  count-of (c) = if c.tag == 3 then 3 else list-length c.pts / 2

  cmd-strengths : List DrawCmd, Integer -> List Real
  cmd-strengths (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else [(list-at cs i).strength] & cmd-strengths cs (i + 1)

 GEOM THEN POINTS. Paint's DrawCmd now keeps a command's own parameters -- the
 beacon's centre and radius here -- in `geom`, and `pts` is always the polygon.
 The beacon has no polygon, so this stream is its geom; every other command here
 has no geom, so it is its points. The wire is unchanged either way.

  cmd-coords : List DrawCmd, Integer -> List Real
  cmd-coords (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else (list-at cs i).geom & (list-at cs i).pts & cmd-coords cs (i + 1)

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-rel  "tw-offset   " (list-map beacon-offset-for offset-ns) g-tw-offset 0.000001)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints "tw-percase  " per-case g-tw-percase)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints "tw-tags     " (cmd-tags all-cmds 0) g-tw-tags)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints "tw-colors   " (cmd-colors all-cmds 0) g-tw-colors)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints "tw-counts   " (list-map count-of all-cmds) g-tw-counts)
    print-line-uni (grade-rel  "tw-strengths" (cmd-strengths all-cmds 0) g-tw-strengths 0.000001)
    print-line-uni (grade-px   "tw-coords   " (cmd-coords all-cmds 0) g-tw-coords 0.001 0.000001)
  end
